INT. RIVERBOAT CALLIOPE ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped brass chamber beneath the smokestacks. Steam pipes, a keyboard of fat ivory keys, a hundred whistles gleaming in the lamplight. Through a port window, black river water slides by.

CAPTAIN PRUDENCE LOCKWOOD, 50s, braided cuffs, a cigar gone cold, leans against the pipes with her arms folded. She watches the boy at the keys.

JUBAL, 17, bare feet, sleeves rolled, sweats at the calliope. He plays a hymn badly, then well, then badly again.

In the corner, TENNYSON, 60s, a gambler in a black frock coat, slouches in a barrel chair with a pistol resting on his knee. He is not listening to the music.

PRUDENCE
Jubal, stop.

The calliope sighs to silence. Steam hisses. Jubal turns, wiping his hands on his shirt.

JUBAL
Yes, Captain.

PRUDENCE
Tell Mr. Tennyson what you told me this morning.

JUBAL
About the crates?

PRUDENCE
About the crates.

TENNYSON
(not moving)
Let the boy speak, Prudence.

PRUDENCE
He's speaking.

JUBAL
I loaded forty-one crates at St. Louis, sir. Marked flour. They came out forty-one at Cairo, marked flour. But the ones at Cairo, they were light.

TENNYSON
Light.

JUBAL
Like they had nothing but air in 'em.

TENNYSON
Flour is light, son.

JUBAL
Not that light.

TENNYSON
How would you know?

JUBAL
I carried three hundred sacks of it for my father before he died.

Tennyson smiles, thin. Studies the pistol.

TENNYSON
Captain, your boy has sharp eyes.

PRUDENCE
He has sharp ears too.

TENNYSON
Which brings me to my question. What did he hear?

JUBAL
Hear?

PRUDENCE
He heard you in the hold, Mr. Tennyson. Last Tuesday. Opening crates.

TENNYSON
Did he.

PRUDENCE
He told me you were counting something. Not flour.

TENNYSON
I count many things, Captain. Ducats. Dealers. Debts.

PRUDENCE
Gold.

A long silence. The river hisses along the hull. Jubal's hands, still on the keys, tremble.

TENNYSON
(softly)
Boy. Play something.

JUBAL
Sir?

TENNYSON
Something loud.

Jubal looks at Prudence. She nods, almost imperceptibly. He turns back and brings both hands down on the keys. The calliope screams into the night, a great, shrieking chord that rattles the pipes.

Tennyson rises. He doesn't raise the pistol. He walks to the port and looks out at the river.

TENNYSON (CONT'D)
(over the noise)
It was a good run, Prudence.

PRUDENCE
(over the noise)
It was.

TENNYSON
You'd have made a fine partner.

PRUDENCE
I made a better captain.

He laughs, once. Tucks the pistol into his coat. Steps through the door and onto the deck. The calliope plays on.

Jubal keeps playing until the last of the steam gives out. In the silence after, he looks up.

JUBAL
Captain?

PRUDENCE
Yes, Jubal?

JUBAL
Was that a hymn?

PRUDENCE
Close enough.
